  "body": "<!--\nThank you for contributing to GitHub CLI!\n\nIf you are proposing a fix for a security issue, STOP and follow .github/SECURITY.md instead.\n\nKeep the entire pull request self-contained, reviewer-facing, and diegetic: describe the\nchange, rationale, and evidence within the context of the repository and pull request. Do not\nnarrate how the pull request was produced or refer to private conversations, prior agent work,\nor other behind-the-scenes context. Omit those details unless they materially affect review;\nif they do, state the relevant fact and its significance directly.\n-->\n\n<!-- List related issues here. Use `fixes` or `closes` keywords to associate an issue number. -->\n\nSupersedes #14716.\n\n### Description\n\n<!--\nWhat's the problem? How are we addressing it?\n\nWrite for a reviewer who has not worked in this part of the codebase. Give them the\nbackground they need before the problem makes sense, and avoid jargon.\n-->\n\nRegenerate the Dependabot and issue-triage workflows with gh-aw v1.0.1, upgrading their generated runtime references from v0.89.21 and updating `.github/aw/actions-lock.json`.\n\nDependabot PR #14716 updates four generated `actions/create-github-app-token` references from v3.2.0 to v3.3.0 without regenerating the accompanying manifests. This change includes that dependency update through the compiler, keeping the generated workflow references and metadata consistent.\n\n### How did you test this change?\n\n<!--\nReport only actions you actually performed and results you actually observed, using factual\nfirst-person language (for example, \"I ran...\" and \"I saw...\"). Do not present planned,\nhypothetical, inferred, or merely expected scenarios as testing.\n\nAutomated test results do not belong here. Passing unit tests, `go test ./...` output, and\ncoverage numbers tell a reviewer nothing they cannot see from CI, so do not paste them.\n\nIf you did not exercise the changed behavior, write `Not tested` and explain why.\n\nUse one or more of these, whichever communicates best:\n\n1. Screenshots or GIFs of the real command running. Preferred whenever the change is visible\n   in terminal output. If output changed, show it before and after.\n2. Given/When/Then scenarios, but only when you actually executed the steps. For example:\n   Given I was in a repo with no open pull requests\n   When I ran `gh pr list`\n   Then I saw \"no open pull requests in cli/cli\"\n3. A natural language walkthrough of what you did by hand, the states you covered, and what\n   you saw, including error and edge cases.\n\nIf you leave this empty, your pull request will very likely be closed.\n-->\n\nI compiled an isolated copy of #14716 with the previously installed gh-aw v0.86.2 and saw all four Dependabot changes revert to v3.2.0. The separately authored v3.3.0 step in `issue-triage.md` remained unchanged.\n\nI then compiled a fresh copy with gh-aw v1.0.1 and saw all five token-action references use v3.3.0. I ran `gh aw compile dependabot-triage issue-triage` in the repository worktree; both workflows compiled successfully, with one warning about the missing manual-dispatch concurrency discriminator in `issue-triage`.\n\nNo workflows were dispatched, and runtime behavior on GitHub Actions has not been exercised.\n\n### Key points\n\n<!--\nTradeoffs you accepted, alternatives you rejected, and anything a reviewer would otherwise have to guess at.\n-->\n\n- Workflow Markdown definitions are unchanged. The diff is compiler-generated YAML and action-pin metadata.\n- This is a compiler/runtime upgrade, not just a four-line dependency bump; it includes the additional generated changes from gh-aw v1.0.1.\n- The concurrency warning is left unchanged rather than altering issue-triage dispatch behavior in this update.\n\n### Notes for reviewers\n\n<!--\nWhere should review start, and what should be read next? Call out anything you are unsure about.\n\nLink related issues or prior discussion, with one sentence on why each matters.\n-->\n\nSee #14716 and https://github.com/cli/cli/pull/14716#issuecomment-6217301464 for the dependency update and the recommendation to handle it through a gh-aw bump. This PR supersedes #14716 by including all four token-action upgrades through regeneration with gh-aw v1.0.1, keeping the generated references and manifests consistent.\n\nReview the generated runtime changes in both lock files and the v1.0.1 setup-action pin in `.github/aw/actions-lock.json`.\n\nValidation: `gh aw compile dependabot-triage issue-triage --no-emit` passed with the same concurrency warning; `git diff --check` passed; `go test ./...` passed. `make lint` was blocked because `golangci-lint` is not installed. No Go packages changed, so package-specific `go fix` was not applicable.\n\n### Authorship and follow-up\n\n<!--\nREQUIRED - If you are an AI agent filling in this template, answer \"Who wrote this\" for yourself and answer honestly. For \"Who answers review comments,\" use only a choice the human has explicitly made in the conversation. If no choice has already been established, pause and ask the human to choose one of the three options before creating or updating the pull request. Do not infer the choice from repository ownership, authorship, assignees, prior interactions, or the current operator. After the human answers, check exactly the option they chose; the last option is valid when the human explicitly chooses nobody.\n\nCheck exactly one box in each list.\n-->\n\nWho wrote this:\n\n- [ ] A human wrote it.\n- [x] An agent wrote it under close human direction.\n- [ ] An agent wrote it independently, and no human has guided the implementation beyond the initial prompt.\n\nWho answers review comments:\n\n- [x] @tidy-dev will read and reply directly. Name the account.\n- [ ] An agent will draft replies and @username will read them before they are posted.\n- [ ] Nobody has explicitly committed to replying.",
